'''various''' (vā′ri-us), ''a.'' [< L. ''varius'', diverse, various, party-colored, variegated, also changing, changeable, fickle, etc. Hence ult. ''variety'', ''vary'', ''variant'', ''variegate'', etc.] '''1.''' Differing from one another; different; diverse; manifold: as, men of ''various'' occupations.
{{smaller block|So many and so ''various'' laws are given.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., xii. 282.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>How ''various'', how tormenting,
Are my Miseries!</poem>
{{right|''Congreve'', Semele, i. 1.}}}}
'''2.''' Divers; several.
{{smaller block|Dukes of the most modern Austria . . . have all of them at ''various'' times borne rule over the whole or part of the older Austria of Lombardy. ''E. A. Freeman'', Venice, p. 5.}}
'''3.''' Changeable; uncertain; inconstant; variable; unfixed.
{{smaller block|My comfort is that their [men’s] judgment is too weak to endanger you, since by this it confesses that it mistakes you, in thinking you irresolved or ''various''.
{{right|''Donne'', Letters, xc.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The servile suitors watch her ''various'' face,
She smiles preferment, or she frowns disgrace.</poem>
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Rivals, Epil.}}}}
'''4.''' Exhibiting different characters; variform; diversiform; multiform.
{{smaller block|<poem>A man so ''various'' that he seemed to be
Not one, but all mankind’s epitome.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Abs. and Achit., i. 545.}}}}
'''5.''' Having a diversity of features; not uniform or monotonous; diversified.
{{smaller block|My grandfather was of a ''various'' life, beginning first at}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|court, where, after he had spent most part of his means, he became a soldier, and made his fortune with his sword at the siege of St. Quintens in France and other wars.
{{right|''Lord Herbert of Cherbury'', Life (ed. Howells), p. 24.}}}}
{{smaller block|A happy rural seat of ''various'' view.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., iv. 247.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>A ''various'' host they came — whose ranks display
Each mode in which the warrior meets the fight.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', Vision of Don Roderick, The Vision, st. 57.}}}}
{{smaller block|It is a common belief that Mr. Webster was a ''various'' reader; and I think it is true.
{{right|''R. Choate'', Addresses, p. 235.}}}}

'''variously''' (vā′ri-us-li), ''adv.'' In various or different ways; diversely; multifariously.

'''variousness''' (vā′ri-us-nes), ''n.'' The character or state of being various; variety; multifariousness.

'''variscite''' (var′i-sīt), ''n.'' [< L. ''Variscia'', Voigtland (now part of Saxony), + ''-ite''<sup>2</sup>.] A hydrous phosphate of aluminium, occurring in crystalline or reniform crusts of a bright-green color.

'''varix''' (vā′riks), ''n.''; pl. ''varices'' (var′i-sēz). [= F. ''varice'' = Sp. ''variz'', ''varice'' = Pg. ''varix'' = It. ''varice'', < L. ''varix'' (''varic-''), a dilated vein, < ''varus'', bent, stretched: see ''varus''.] '''1.''' Abnormal dilatation or tortuosity of a vein or other vessel of the body; also, a vein, artery, or lymphatic thus dilated or tortuous; a varicose vessel.—'''2.''' [NL.] In ''conch.'', a mark or scar on the surface of a shell denoting a former position of the lip of the aperture, which

readings.c2s1:
has passed on with the periodical growth of the shell. Varices are conspicuous in some univalves. See cuts under ''murex'' and ''triton''. —'''Aneurismal varix.''' {{smaller|See ''aneurismal''.}} —'''Lymphatic varix,''' {{smaller|dilatation of the lymphatic vessels.}}

